1
00:00:07,666 --> 00:00:09,833
[engine starts, roars]

2
00:00:19,566 --> 00:00:23,266
[Jerry] This is a 1963 Corvette Stingray 
in silver blue,

3
00:00:23,333 --> 00:00:27,533
with a blue interior
and kind of a dark purplish upholstery.

4
00:00:27,599 --> 00:00:28,433
Killer.

5
00:00:31,333 --> 00:00:33,533
It's got a 327 cubic inch V8,

6
00:00:33,633 --> 00:00:36,633
four-barrel carb,
and proper knock-off wheels.

7
00:00:39,199 --> 00:00:41,033
Are there faster Corvettes? Yes.

8
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:43,866
Are there bigger-engine,
tire-melting Corvettes?

9
00:00:43,933 --> 00:00:44,900
Of course.

10
00:00:44,966 --> 00:00:46,566
Are there cooler Corvettes?

11
00:00:47,699 --> 00:00:49,099
I don't think so.

12
00:00:51,933 --> 00:00:55,333
1963 is the only year
with the split rear window.

13
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:57,266
This car looks like a submarine.

14
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:01,066
Like you could drive
right into the water and keep going.

15
00:01:02,266 --> 00:01:06,100
It's edgy, it's stubby,
it's perfectly proportioned,

16
00:01:06,166 --> 00:01:09,600
with just the right amount of detail
and just the right stance.

17
00:01:11,333 --> 00:01:12,533
In the '60s and '70s,

18
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:15,766
all American astronauts
in the space program drove Corvettes.

19
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:19,433
When you drive a Saturn V at work,

20
00:01:20,033 --> 00:01:23,033
you cannot get out of that
and into a Country Squire wagon.

21
00:01:24,366 --> 00:01:26,966
Look at the scoops. Look at the nose.
Look at the tail.

22
00:01:27,066 --> 00:01:29,899
Look at the door cutouts into the roof.

23
00:01:29,966 --> 00:01:32,966
The idea behind this car
is just confidence.

24
00:01:33,066 --> 00:01:36,800
This came from an American culture
that would just say things and do them

25
00:01:36,866 --> 00:01:39,166
without having any idea how.

26
00:01:39,233 --> 00:01:40,666
"We're gonna put a man on the moon."

27
00:01:40,733 --> 00:01:42,666
"How we gonna do it?
When we gonna do it?

28
00:01:42,733 --> 00:01:44,933
Why are we doing this?" "No idea."

29
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,866
We just did stuff. Crazy, but great.

30
00:01:49,066 --> 00:01:51,800
And I think this cool blue
is just the right color

31
00:01:51,866 --> 00:01:55,033
for my are-you-kidding-me
super-special guest today,

32
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:59,000
the commander in chief
of the United States of America,

33
00:01:59,066 --> 00:02:00,666
President Barack Obama...

34
00:02:01,633 --> 00:02:02,899
who, in my opinion,

35
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,633
has gotten off just enough funny lines
to qualify for getting on this show.

36
00:02:08,166 --> 00:02:10,799
A few weeks ago,
Dick Cheney says he thinks

37
00:02:10,866 --> 00:02:13,500
I'm the worst president of his lifetime.

38
00:02:13,566 --> 00:02:14,700
[audience laughs]

39
00:02:14,766 --> 00:02:16,366
Which is interesting, because I think

40
00:02:16,433 --> 00:02:19,133
Dick Cheney is the worst president
of my lifetime.

41
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:20,200
[audience laughing]

42
00:02:20,566 --> 00:02:22,799
-[telephone ringing]
-[man, over phone] White House.

43
00:02:22,866 --> 00:02:25,133
[Jerry] Yes, may I speak
with the president, please?

44
00:02:25,178 --> 00:02:27,247
-[Obama] Speaking.
-[Jerry laughs]

45
00:02:27,900 --> 00:02:32,366
[Jerry] Sir, may I compliment you on
cutting all that red tape in Washington.

46
00:02:32,752 --> 00:02:34,087
So you still up for a coffee?

47
00:02:34,154 --> 00:02:37,123
[Obama] Sure. Why don't you come on by?
It's 1600 Pennsylvania.

48
00:02:37,666 --> 00:02:39,533
[Jerry] Yeah. I know.

49
00:02:41,366 --> 00:02:46,500
I'm Jerry Seinfeld, and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

50
00:02:54,733 --> 00:02:56,266
[Jerry whistling]

51
00:02:56,333 --> 00:02:57,466
[leaves rustling]

52
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:15,328
[Jerry] You ready?

53
00:03:15,995 --> 00:03:17,463
I got some stuff to do.

54
00:03:19,733 --> 00:03:20,866
[Jerry sighs]

55
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:35,766
Are these washed?

56
00:03:35,966 --> 00:03:38,299
[Obama chuckling]

57
00:03:39,986 --> 00:03:42,188
Come on, let's go.
Let's go get some coffee.

58
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:46,266
-Well, don't leave the apple, though...
-[Jerry chuckles]

59
00:03:46,359 --> 00:03:48,294
If you're gonna have a bite,
you've gotta take it.

60
00:03:48,394 --> 00:03:51,064
[Jerry] Okay. Can I throw this out?
You have a garbage?

61
00:03:51,166 --> 00:03:53,133
You have to use
the non-presidential garbage.

62
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:54,133
Oh, I see.

63
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:55,466
-[Jerry laughs]
-[Obama] Come on.

64
00:03:55,568 --> 00:03:57,704
[inaudible]

65
00:03:58,833 --> 00:04:01,366
-This is a sweet car right here.
-Okay, now check this out.

66
00:04:01,433 --> 00:04:02,466
This is beautiful.

67
00:04:02,533 --> 00:04:05,299
I thought, the coolest car,

68
00:04:05,399 --> 00:04:06,600
American-made,

69
00:04:06,700 --> 00:04:09,333
for the coolest guy
ever to hold this office.

70
00:04:09,399 --> 00:04:10,533
[Obama] I...

71
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:13,366
You know what? You are winning
a lot of points with me right now.

72
00:04:13,419 --> 00:04:14,887
[inaudible]

73
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:17,899
A friend of my grandfather's,

74
00:04:18,124 --> 00:04:21,160
who had this when I was, like,

75
00:04:21,294 --> 00:04:23,596
-I was what, seven or eight or something.
-Right.

76
00:04:24,133 --> 00:04:26,733
And I remember seeing this thing
and thinking,

77
00:04:27,166 --> 00:04:29,800
if I was ever able
to get me one of these...

78
00:04:29,866 --> 00:04:31,400
-Yeah.
-...I'd be set.

79
00:04:31,966 --> 00:04:34,000
-Well, let's hop in. Come on.
-Let's go.

80
00:04:34,099 --> 00:04:35,599
I'm glad you had some free time.

81
00:04:36,099 --> 00:04:37,666
I really like this car.

82
00:04:44,933 --> 00:04:47,099
-Be careful about hitting, uh...
-Oh.

83
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:49,099
-[Obama] You look nervous.
-I'm a little nervous.

84
00:04:49,166 --> 00:04:51,599
-Are you always this nervous?
-No, this is a little different.

85
00:04:51,658 --> 00:04:54,093
-Like with Fallon and Letterman?
-It's not the same.

86
00:04:54,166 --> 00:04:56,333
-No. It's not the same.
-It's not as stressful.

87
00:04:57,397 --> 00:04:59,465
[Jerry] We're just gonna run out
for a quick coffee.

88
00:04:59,532 --> 00:05:01,601
-We'll be right back.
-I'm sorry. That's not possible.

89
00:05:01,666 --> 00:05:04,433
I do this little show,
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

90
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:06,400
You're a comedian with the president
going nowhere.

91
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:07,933
-Back it up.
-Yes, sir.

92
00:05:12,466 --> 00:05:14,133
[Jerry] Do you ever think

93
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:19,033
about every person you talk to
is putting on an act, a total show?

94
00:05:19,700 --> 00:05:21,166
-It's a problem.
-[Jerry laughs]

95
00:05:21,866 --> 00:05:25,200
Don't you think every American child
is a president fan?

96
00:05:25,300 --> 00:05:27,733
You grow up loving the president.

97
00:05:27,794 --> 00:05:31,397
I do really well
with the zero to eight demographic.

98
00:05:31,464 --> 00:05:32,899
-Oh, really?
-Yeah. They love me.

99
00:05:32,966 --> 00:05:35,300
-Partly 'cause I think my ears are big.
-[Jerry laughing]

100
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:37,766
And so I look
a little like a cartoon character.

101
00:05:37,804 --> 00:05:41,741
-Right.
-And then little kids love saying my name.

102
00:05:42,166 --> 00:05:44,733
-Right. That's funny.
-But it's all one big name.

103
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:46,200
It's Barackobama.

104
00:05:46,266 --> 00:05:49,533
[Jerry] It is one of those names
that you could compress into one word.

105
00:05:49,582 --> 00:05:51,117
-One word. Barackobama.
-It's good.

106
00:05:51,184 --> 00:05:52,785
It's like a Nickelodeon thing.

107
00:05:52,866 --> 00:05:55,000
[Jerry] Well, anything "orama" is fun.

108
00:05:55,066 --> 00:05:56,400
[Obama laughing]

109
00:05:56,966 --> 00:05:59,099
This is called The Beast.

110
00:05:59,733 --> 00:06:03,233
It's a Caddy, basically on a tank frame.

111
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,766
-[Jerry] Oh, my God.
-Yeah, see?

112
00:06:06,833 --> 00:06:10,203
So, I could call a nuclear submarine
right here from this...

113
00:06:10,269 --> 00:06:12,538
-I'll bet you don't have that in your car.
-I don't have that.

114
00:06:12,633 --> 00:06:14,066
Yeah, it's a cool feature.

115
00:06:14,133 --> 00:06:15,366
Plus, seat warmers.

116
00:06:15,433 --> 00:06:18,533
-So, between the nuclear submarine thing--
-Well, everybody's got that. Yeah.

117
00:06:18,633 --> 00:06:19,966
Is that right? That's standard now?

118
00:06:20,033 --> 00:06:22,033
That's pretty standard
everywhere now, yeah.

119
00:06:22,133 --> 00:06:24,000
[both laughing]

120
00:06:24,099 --> 00:06:26,400
This I like, though. I'm gonna get that.

121
00:06:27,433 --> 00:06:29,033
You can't have that.

122
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:33,533
What is it that you really want to do
that they don't let you do?

123
00:06:33,599 --> 00:06:36,866
I would love to just be taking a walk,

124
00:06:36,933 --> 00:06:40,633
and then I run into you,
you're sitting on a bench. Right?

125
00:06:40,733 --> 00:06:42,101
-Now I see--
-And suddenly I say,

126
00:06:42,168 --> 00:06:44,637
"Hey, Jerry, how you doing?"
You say, "I'm doing pretty good.

127
00:06:44,704 --> 00:06:47,206
-What are you doing?"
-"Nothing. It's a Saturday morning."

128
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:48,599
Right.

129
00:06:48,866 --> 00:06:51,233
-That moment when you lost your anonymity.
-Mmm-hmm.

130
00:06:51,299 --> 00:06:56,066
And anonymity is not something
you think about as being valuable.

131
00:06:56,933 --> 00:06:58,533
-With all due respect...
-Yeah.

132
00:06:58,599 --> 00:07:00,733
I remember very well not being famous.

133
00:07:00,833 --> 00:07:04,366
-You think it was okay?
-It wasn't that great.

134
00:07:04,433 --> 00:07:05,433
-Yeah?
-It was...

135
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:07,200
You think being famous is better?

136
00:07:09,433 --> 00:07:10,633
-Yes?
-Yeah.

137
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:12,433
[both laughing]

138
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:14,799
I always wanted to be
in a show about nothing,

139
00:07:14,866 --> 00:07:15,933
and here I am.

140
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,233
There's nothing
more nothing than this. Nothing.

141
00:07:19,299 --> 00:07:20,700
Come on, let's go get some coffee.

142
00:07:22,241 --> 00:07:25,211
[Jerry] So, when you were a kid,
did you have favorite presidents?

143
00:07:25,333 --> 00:07:27,966
Teddy Roosevelt is a cool character.

144
00:07:28,033 --> 00:07:32,933
In fact, he may be the guy who
would be the most fun to hang out with.

145
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,766
He just does crazy stuff.

146
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:38,299
[Obama] Mmm.

147
00:07:39,066 --> 00:07:41,733
-[Jerry] So--
-That's just a little, a little gin.

148
00:07:41,799 --> 00:07:43,133
-[Jerry laughs]
-I take midday.

149
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:44,266
You were asking...

150
00:07:44,333 --> 00:07:46,099
You were asking what part of the day,

151
00:07:46,166 --> 00:07:47,466
-what part of the day--
-Yeah, when--

152
00:07:47,533 --> 00:07:49,900
-It's right about now.
-Yeah. That gives you a little zip?

153
00:07:51,333 --> 00:07:55,200
[Obama] Teddy Roosevelt would go up to
Yellowstone Park for, like, a month,

154
00:07:55,266 --> 00:07:58,233
and nobody knew where he was,
nobody could get in touch with him.

155
00:07:58,533 --> 00:08:00,799
-Can you imagine that?
-Wait a minute. In office?

156
00:08:00,866 --> 00:08:03,700
In office. Sounds pretty good to me.

157
00:08:03,983 --> 00:08:06,319
[Jerry] Boy, that's a lot of messages
when you get back.

158
00:08:11,533 --> 00:08:13,266
-How do you get up?
-[Obama] Uh...

159
00:08:13,366 --> 00:08:15,366
-Do you need an alarm?
-Somebody calls me.

160
00:08:15,700 --> 00:08:17,033
You get a wake-up call?

161
00:08:17,099 --> 00:08:18,200
Yeah. Why not?

162
00:08:19,565 --> 00:08:20,666
[Jerry] Get out of bed--

163
00:08:20,733 --> 00:08:24,766
So, I brush my teeth, shave,
put on some workout clothes.

164
00:08:24,866 --> 00:08:26,433
I go up, work out.

165
00:08:26,533 --> 00:08:27,966
Shave, then work out.

166
00:08:28,033 --> 00:08:30,500
Yeah, I believe in shaving
before the workout.

167
00:08:30,766 --> 00:08:31,599
Okay.

168
00:08:31,666 --> 00:08:36,500
Because that's how I do it,
and I don't really need a reason.

169
00:08:36,566 --> 00:08:37,433
[Jerry] I see.

170
00:08:41,033 --> 00:08:42,566
I guess I'll just make some myself.

171
00:08:42,633 --> 00:08:43,799
Let's see you make coffee.

172
00:08:49,466 --> 00:08:52,933
How far can you wander around up there
in your underwear?

173
00:08:53,066 --> 00:08:56,400
How far can you get before there's,
like, people and it's not cool?

174
00:08:56,966 --> 00:09:00,100
It's not cool, generally,
wandering around in my underwear.

175
00:09:01,266 --> 00:09:02,966
Can you really get a good night's...

176
00:09:03,033 --> 00:09:05,600
Isn't this like Night at the Museum
when you go to sleep here?

177
00:09:05,666 --> 00:09:06,500
First night was.

178
00:09:06,566 --> 00:09:12,366
Of all the jarring elements
of first being elected president.

179
00:09:12,433 --> 00:09:14,233
You know, you've got the inauguration,

180
00:09:14,287 --> 00:09:16,555
and there are two million people,
and there are parades,

181
00:09:16,622 --> 00:09:19,392
and there's this and there's that,
all the hoopla.

182
00:09:19,466 --> 00:09:24,400
The thing that really does get to you
is, after all that's over,

183
00:09:24,764 --> 00:09:30,236
and it's the first night,
and you're sleeping in the White House.

184
00:09:30,333 --> 00:09:32,166
-Right. Right.
-It's not like a hotel room.

185
00:09:32,233 --> 00:09:33,066
Right.

186
00:09:33,133 --> 00:09:35,000
So, you're looking around
and you're thinking,

187
00:09:35,066 --> 00:09:36,533
-"What the hell is this?"
-[laughs]

188
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:37,666
-Right?
-Yeah.

189
00:09:37,733 --> 00:09:38,833
-Night at the Museum.
-Yeah.

190
00:09:38,933 --> 00:09:41,800
That's how it feels
probably the first week.

191
00:09:41,866 --> 00:09:44,633
There's a lot of crazy stuff
that's happened in this house.

192
00:09:44,699 --> 00:09:46,133
There is, yeah.

193
00:09:46,199 --> 00:09:48,333
Does that ever enter your mind?

194
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:49,733
-Sometimes it does.
-Yeah.

195
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:53,066
There are times where you think,

196
00:09:53,159 --> 00:09:55,261
"Okay, here's where

197
00:09:55,328 --> 00:09:56,796
-Kennedy was hanging out."
-Right.

198
00:09:56,900 --> 00:10:00,033
Or "Here's where Nixon
was talking to Haldeman

199
00:10:00,100 --> 00:10:02,633
-about whatever they were talking about."
-Right. Yeah, yeah.

200
00:10:03,035 --> 00:10:05,605
Can you adjust the temperature
in here at all?

201
00:10:05,671 --> 00:10:08,174
Is there a therm-- Have you ever
touched a thermostat in here?

202
00:10:08,240 --> 00:10:10,209
No, I make a call.

203
00:10:10,300 --> 00:10:11,400
You make a call. [laughs]

204
00:10:14,866 --> 00:10:17,800
If I slid open your underwear drawer,

205
00:10:17,866 --> 00:10:20,366
one brand or a number of brands?

206
00:10:20,433 --> 00:10:22,966
-You gotta go with one brand.
-One brand. One color?

207
00:10:23,389 --> 00:10:25,625
Yeah, of course.
This is a critical concept.

208
00:10:25,699 --> 00:10:27,666
It's more work that you don't need.

209
00:10:27,733 --> 00:10:30,500
How many suits do they put out
in the morning?

210
00:10:30,563 --> 00:10:33,632
Well, they don't put out suits.
I have a closet like normal people.

211
00:10:33,733 --> 00:10:35,066
-You pick the suit?
-I do.

212
00:10:35,133 --> 00:10:36,533
[chuckles] Yes.

213
00:10:36,633 --> 00:10:37,600
-Really?
-I do. I do.

214
00:10:37,666 --> 00:10:40,366
I would have a guy just leave the...
Same with the lunch.

215
00:10:40,433 --> 00:10:42,166
Here's the suit, here's the lunch.

216
00:10:42,233 --> 00:10:46,233
Well, with the suit,
I only have blue and gray suits.

217
00:10:46,300 --> 00:10:48,266
-Right. No black?
-I have one black suit.

218
00:10:48,333 --> 00:10:50,633
Black's too rough for a president.
You gotta soften it a little.

219
00:10:50,699 --> 00:10:53,166
-Well, but I'm a cool president, so--
-Right. That's true.

220
00:10:54,900 --> 00:10:58,800
What's the coolest
president commemoration?

221
00:10:59,266 --> 00:11:01,333
-Oh, okay. That's interesting.
-To get?

222
00:11:01,400 --> 00:11:04,033
'Cause you're in line
to get something great.

223
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:05,466
The Rushmore thing...

224
00:11:05,533 --> 00:11:07,333
-Rushmore?
-...would be interesting,

225
00:11:07,400 --> 00:11:10,633
but that's pretty exclusive real estate.

226
00:11:10,699 --> 00:11:12,466
You know what I noticed about
Mount Rushmore?

227
00:11:12,533 --> 00:11:16,100
Washington has totally turned his back
on those other three guys.

228
00:11:16,166 --> 00:11:17,100
[laughing]

229
00:11:17,166 --> 00:11:18,199
[stammers] I--

230
00:11:18,300 --> 00:11:20,133
-He is in a different angle.
-Yeah.

231
00:11:21,833 --> 00:11:24,866
Have you ever had one of those
George W. Bush...

232
00:11:24,933 --> 00:11:27,633
Remember when he couldn't
find the door in the wall?

233
00:11:27,699 --> 00:11:30,066
-Yeah.
-And he was just looking for the door?

234
00:11:30,133 --> 00:11:33,400
I don't ever believe
that publicly you've had one of those.

235
00:11:33,466 --> 00:11:35,266
I have not had a public moment like that.

236
00:11:35,466 --> 00:11:37,333
-You haven't tumbled down a stair?
-No. Not yet.

237
00:11:37,400 --> 00:11:39,966
You never leaned over
and just started vomiting in Japan?

238
00:11:40,033 --> 00:11:42,866
-No. Nothing like that's happened so far.
-Nothing?

239
00:11:42,966 --> 00:11:45,533
What's your most embarrassing
president moment?

240
00:11:46,400 --> 00:11:47,633
This may be it.

241
00:11:47,699 --> 00:11:48,766
[Jerry laughs]

242
00:11:49,809 --> 00:11:53,412
You're one of those guys,
even though you seem very relaxed,

243
00:11:53,500 --> 00:11:56,433
you gotta go off at some point with food.

244
00:11:56,533 --> 00:11:58,466
-What's your thing--
-Nachos.

245
00:11:58,533 --> 00:11:59,699
Would a tray of-- Nachos.

246
00:12:00,019 --> 00:12:02,722
That's one of those
where I have to have it taken away.

247
00:12:03,389 --> 00:12:05,357
-I'll have guacamole...
-It's the greatest.

248
00:12:05,424 --> 00:12:06,992
-...coming out of my eyeballs.
-Yeah.

249
00:12:08,894 --> 00:12:11,363
People you spend most of your time with,

250
00:12:12,031 --> 00:12:14,066
are they really smart?

251
00:12:14,133 --> 00:12:19,100
Or are they mostly headstrong,
agenda-laden idiots?

252
00:12:19,166 --> 00:12:22,800
You know, when you're
dealing with Congress, uh, it varies.

253
00:12:22,866 --> 00:12:25,199
There are gonna be some folks there
that are foolish.

254
00:12:25,266 --> 00:12:26,300
Mmm-hmm.

255
00:12:26,633 --> 00:12:30,300
-Just like there are in comedy or...
-Mmm-hmm. Mmm-hmm.

256
00:12:30,400 --> 00:12:32,966
Well, everyone in comedy is foolish.

257
00:12:33,033 --> 00:12:34,733
All my friends are knuckleheads.

258
00:12:34,833 --> 00:12:36,066
-All of 'em?
-All of 'em.

259
00:12:36,166 --> 00:12:37,066
I know some of your friends.

260
00:12:37,166 --> 00:12:39,199
Did I tell you I played golf
with Larry David?

261
00:12:39,900 --> 00:12:42,133
No, 'cause you and I don't talk that much.

262
00:12:42,633 --> 00:12:43,599
I love Larry.

263
00:12:43,699 --> 00:12:46,800
When we play golf,
he's a fair-skinned guy, which is--

264
00:12:46,866 --> 00:12:48,533
[groans] The sunscreen.

265
00:12:48,633 --> 00:12:50,766
-He lathers himself in sunscreen.
-I can't take it, I know.

266
00:12:50,833 --> 00:12:51,766
It's gross.

267
00:12:51,833 --> 00:12:55,566
It's dripping,
and it's caked white all over.

268
00:12:55,641 --> 00:12:57,276
It catches parts of his ears,

269
00:12:57,343 --> 00:12:59,111
-and there's globs of it.
-It's horrible. Yeah.

270
00:13:00,833 --> 00:13:03,433
What sport is politics?

271
00:13:03,599 --> 00:13:06,366
Is it chess? Is it liar's poker?

272
00:13:06,866 --> 00:13:09,633
That's interesting.
That was a good question.

273
00:13:10,500 --> 00:13:12,033
It's probably most like football.

274
00:13:12,099 --> 00:13:13,033
-Football?
-Yeah.

275
00:13:13,133 --> 00:13:15,433
Because a lot of players,

276
00:13:15,966 --> 00:13:19,300
-a lot of specialization, lot of hitting.
-Right.

277
00:13:19,366 --> 00:13:21,566
-Lot of attrition.
-A lot of attrition.

278
00:13:21,966 --> 00:13:26,266
Um, but then, every once in a while,
you'll see an opening.

279
00:13:26,333 --> 00:13:29,033
-Uh-huh.
-Right? So you're gonna be...

280
00:13:29,099 --> 00:13:31,300
You hit the line, you get one yard.

281
00:13:31,366 --> 00:13:34,199
-Right.
-You try a play, you get sacked.

282
00:13:34,266 --> 00:13:37,900
-Now it's, like, third and 15.
-Mmm-hmm.

283
00:13:37,966 --> 00:13:41,133
But every once in a while...
You have to punt a lot.

284
00:13:41,233 --> 00:13:43,566
But every once in a while,
you'll see a hole,

285
00:13:43,633 --> 00:13:45,000
and then there's open field.

286
00:13:47,800 --> 00:13:53,333
How many world leaders do you think
are just completely out of their mind?

287
00:13:53,866 --> 00:13:54,966
A pretty sizable percent.

288
00:13:55,033 --> 00:13:56,199
[laughing]

289
00:13:56,266 --> 00:13:57,833
Some of these people, you must meet them,

290
00:13:57,900 --> 00:14:00,233
you'll just be chatting,
and you see in the eyes.

291
00:14:00,300 --> 00:14:03,900
You look in the eyes, you go,
"Oh, this guy's gone."

292
00:14:03,966 --> 00:14:05,966
Yeah. Well, and part of what happens is

293
00:14:06,066 --> 00:14:10,366
these guys,
I think, the longer they stay in office,

294
00:14:11,699 --> 00:14:14,366
the more likely that is to happen.

295
00:14:14,466 --> 00:14:15,300
-Of course.
-Right?

296
00:14:15,366 --> 00:14:16,599
-They lose it.
-Yeah.

297
00:14:16,666 --> 00:14:22,166
I mean, at a certain point, your feet hurt
and you're having trouble peeing...

298
00:14:22,333 --> 00:14:25,366
-[Jerry laughs]
-...you have absolute power and--

299
00:14:25,466 --> 00:14:28,000
-Privilege is toxic.
-Yeah.

300
00:14:28,066 --> 00:14:29,933
-Sadly. Sadly.
-It really is.

301
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:31,966
-Things that people struggle to achieve--
-Yeah.

302
00:14:32,099 --> 00:14:34,833
They get to positions of power,
influence, money,

303
00:14:34,900 --> 00:14:35,966
-can do things...
-Right.

304
00:14:36,066 --> 00:14:38,333
It has a toxic effect on their judgment.

305
00:14:38,400 --> 00:14:40,633
Yes. Has that happened to you yet?

306
00:14:40,699 --> 00:14:42,733
-No.
-Why is that do you think?

307
00:14:42,948 --> 00:14:45,551
-Let me ask you some questions.
-Yeah, why don't you ask me some--

308
00:14:45,633 --> 00:14:46,966
Yeah, come on. You do some work.

309
00:14:47,033 --> 00:14:49,333
-So, you're a funny guy.
-Thank you.

310
00:14:49,400 --> 00:14:52,266
You go on some late-night talk shows,

311
00:14:52,333 --> 00:14:54,533
people think you're funny,
they give you a show.

312
00:14:54,599 --> 00:14:57,133
-Right.
-And then next thing you know,

313
00:14:57,199 --> 00:14:58,933
you've made a ridiculous amount of money.

314
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:00,566
So much more than you.

315
00:15:01,133 --> 00:15:03,866
And yet, how do I seem to you?

316
00:15:03,933 --> 00:15:06,400
Do I seem spoiled, out-of-touch?

317
00:15:06,466 --> 00:15:07,533
Well, I don't know.

318
00:15:07,599 --> 00:15:09,233
You have a pretty good instinct
for people.

319
00:15:09,333 --> 00:15:11,433
Right now,
you seem like a completely normal guy.

320
00:15:11,533 --> 00:15:14,433
But I'm putting on an act,
like everyone else does for you.

321
00:15:14,500 --> 00:15:15,833
-That's my point.
-[Jerry laughs]

322
00:15:15,900 --> 00:15:19,400
That's what I'm getting at,
but I'm gonna probe this.

323
00:15:19,718 --> 00:15:21,420
Uh, the question is,

324
00:15:21,533 --> 00:15:26,766
how did you calibrate
dealing with that? Right?

325
00:15:26,833 --> 00:15:27,966
-Well--
-At a certain point,

326
00:15:28,033 --> 00:15:30,000
you might have thought to yourself,
"You know what,

327
00:15:30,099 --> 00:15:31,566
-I'm more than just a comedian."
-Nah.

328
00:15:31,633 --> 00:15:35,133
"I'm gonna make a Jewish version
of Citizen Kane."

329
00:15:35,367 --> 00:15:37,202
You know? How did you keep perspective?

330
00:15:37,300 --> 00:15:38,666
-I'll give you the real answer.
-Yeah.

331
00:15:38,733 --> 00:15:40,699
It's gotta be similar to your life.

332
00:15:40,766 --> 00:15:43,300
I fell in love with the work,
and the work--

333
00:15:43,366 --> 00:15:44,900
-Saved you.
-Was joyful--

334
00:15:44,966 --> 00:15:47,533
-Yeah.
-And difficult and interesting,

335
00:15:47,599 --> 00:15:49,266
and that was my focus.

336
00:15:49,366 --> 00:15:53,566
So, now that you're like
a quasi-retired man of leisure.

337
00:15:53,619 --> 00:15:54,920
-I work a lot.
-Do you?

338
00:15:54,987 --> 00:15:56,855
-Yeah.
-Are you still doing stand-up?

339
00:15:59,400 --> 00:16:01,400
Are you still making speeches?

340
00:16:01,466 --> 00:16:02,500
Do you still get hecklers?

341
00:16:02,566 --> 00:16:04,566
-What's your theory of handling hecklers?
-Sympathy.

342
00:16:04,633 --> 00:16:06,333
I say, "You know what? You seem upset."

343
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:07,233
[laughing]

344
00:16:07,300 --> 00:16:10,333
"I'm so sorry.
I know that's not why you came in here.

345
00:16:10,402 --> 00:16:11,670
-Let's talk."
-[laughing]

346
00:16:13,400 --> 00:16:16,000
What kind of language do people use?

347
00:16:16,066 --> 00:16:17,466
-What kind of language--
-English.

348
00:16:19,666 --> 00:16:21,133
-As a general rule.
-Yeah.

349
00:16:21,199 --> 00:16:23,000
-That's funny.
-[laughing]

350
00:16:23,066 --> 00:16:25,066
Does it help with your stress level?

351
00:16:25,133 --> 00:16:26,699
-'Cause it helps mine.
-I curse.

352
00:16:26,766 --> 00:16:27,833
-"This son of a bitch."
-I curse.

353
00:16:27,900 --> 00:16:30,766
-I say...
-"This rat bastard comes in here..."

354
00:16:31,633 --> 00:16:33,533
-You blow off a little steam, right?
-Yeah.

355
00:16:33,826 --> 00:16:39,031
Bad stuff or stupid stuff
is happening constantly, right? Every day.

356
00:16:39,099 --> 00:16:42,466
So, you have to be able
to just make fun of...

357
00:16:42,566 --> 00:16:45,233
-"Wow, that..."
-Yeah, of course.

358
00:16:45,300 --> 00:16:49,500
"That was even dumber
and more annoying than usual."

359
00:16:49,566 --> 00:16:53,666
That's when, uh...
cursing is really valuable, right?

360
00:16:55,466 --> 00:16:56,433
All right. We just--

361
00:16:56,533 --> 00:16:57,800
-Are we done?
-We're about done.

362
00:16:57,866 --> 00:16:59,566
Only problem is
I didn't work in Obamacare.

363
00:16:59,633 --> 00:17:02,566
Usually, the only reason I do these things
is 'cause I'm promoting health care.

364
00:17:02,633 --> 00:17:04,333
Yeah... But if you want, go ahead.

365
00:17:04,399 --> 00:17:08,033
No. I mean,
if you don't want to sully your show...

366
00:17:08,099 --> 00:17:09,933
I don't mind. I really don't mind.

367
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:13,500
...with the interest of getting people
with no health care,

368
00:17:13,766 --> 00:17:17,736
signing up, so that, heaven forbid,
something happens to 'em--

369
00:17:17,833 --> 00:17:20,966
It's a great thing.
Please try Obamacare today.

370
00:17:21,033 --> 00:17:22,233
[laughing]

371
00:17:24,433 --> 00:17:25,366
That was fun.

372
00:17:26,578 --> 00:17:28,580
[inaudible]

373
00:17:28,714 --> 00:17:30,949
I want to give you a shot
to drive this car.

374
00:17:31,016 --> 00:17:32,851
You know what, this is a childhood dream.

375
00:17:32,933 --> 00:17:34,666
This is outstanding.

376
00:17:34,733 --> 00:17:35,899
Yeah.

377
00:17:35,966 --> 00:17:37,133
You ever go on eBay?

378
00:17:38,133 --> 00:17:39,100
[Obama] No.

379
00:17:39,700 --> 00:17:41,100
Let's do this then.

380
00:17:42,100 --> 00:17:43,633
-Yeah, baby!
-[Jerry laughing]

381
00:17:43,962 --> 00:17:45,831
-Watch out, people.
-[Jerry laughs]

382
00:17:50,700 --> 00:17:52,266
-All righty.
-That's great.

383
00:17:53,933 --> 00:17:57,800
I like the hand hanging over the wheel.

384
00:17:57,843 --> 00:17:59,378
Yeah, yeah. No, I mean, that's...

385
00:17:59,445 --> 00:18:01,680
If you're driving a 'Vette,
gotta be doing it that way.

386
00:18:01,766 --> 00:18:03,766
-You can't be doing the...
-Yeah, no, no, no.

387
00:18:03,866 --> 00:18:06,466
...the "12 and 2" thing.
No, that's not cool. Come on, man.

388
00:18:06,518 --> 00:18:07,753
I'm appreciably cooler

389
00:18:07,820 --> 00:18:09,588
-than I was just two minutes ago.
-Yeah.

390
00:18:14,800 --> 00:18:16,866
I bet if you talk to the guy,
they'll let us out.

391
00:18:17,433 --> 00:18:21,533
Just say, you know, "I'm the president,
and I'm gonna be out for a quick coffee."

392
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:22,466
Hey, Darrell,

393
00:18:22,533 --> 00:18:24,666
-can you open this up--
-Sir, I'm sorry, I can't allow it.

394
00:18:24,733 --> 00:18:25,700
That's unbelievable.

395
00:18:27,033 --> 00:18:27,966
Golly.

396
00:18:32,778 --> 00:18:34,179
I knew that wouldn't work.

397
00:18:34,346 --> 00:18:35,714
You didn't sell it.

398
00:18:46,725 --> 00:18:48,494
He's gonna be lurking
through the bushes?

399
00:18:48,560 --> 00:18:51,263
-[woman] He's gonna be peeking at you.
-Harry, don't, uh...

400
00:18:51,330 --> 00:18:52,464
-Don't shoot him.
-I won't.

401
00:18:52,798 --> 00:18:55,134
He's a very funny guy. We all like him.

402
00:18:55,433 --> 00:18:58,866
My poll numbers would go down
if you did harm to Jerry Seinfeld.

